   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the state finance law, the tax law and the administrative code of the city of New York, in relation to creating the New York city parks, tree and urban forestry fund",
    "basePrintNo": "S7657",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7657-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §99-r, St Fin L; add §623, Tax L; add §18-104.1, NYC Ad Cd",
    "lawSection": "State Finance Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7657\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the state finance law, the tax law and the\nadministrative code of the city of New York, in relation to creating\nthe New York city parks, tree and urban forestry fund\n\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\nTo establish the New York City parks, trees and urban forestry fund\nand to provide funding by means of a check off on city income tax\nreturns. This fund would be used exclusively for the purpose outlined\nin Article 53 of the Environmental Conservation Laws of 1978.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\nThis bill establishes legislativefindings and intent for funding a New\nYork city parks, tree and urban forestry fund and it authorizes the\nState Comptroller and the Commissioner of Taxation and Finance to\njointly establish this special fund. It shall be the duty of the\ncommissioner of the department of parks and recreation of New York\nCity to ensure that such moneys are spent only for the purposes\nstated. Moneys of such ftmd shall be made available to the\ncommissioner of the department of parks and recreation of New York\ncity, upon such submission of certified vouchers and shall be used\nonly for the planting of trees and shrubs, park preservation and\nexpansion, maintenance and improvement of botanical gardens in New\nYork city and to further effectuate the stated purposes of article 53\nof the environmental conservation law. The commissioner shall make\navailable up to,but not more than, fifteen percent of the total amount\nin the fund for the maintenance and improvement of botanical gardens\nin New York City.\n\nThis bill amends the tax law to provide for a Gift for New York City\nparks, trees and urban forestry fund to be placed on the personal\nincome tax return section of the New York City income tax form.\nIndividuals residing in New York City may elect to contribute to this\nfund by having a refund credited to the fund or by indicating that an\noverpayment of taxes owed is to be credited to the fund.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nIn recent years there is an increasing awareness of world wide global\nwarming and the effect of the carbon dioxide emissions on that global\nwarming. Six of the hottest years on record occurred in the 1980's.\nWinters here in the Northeast have become warmer and drier. It is\ncommon for television weather reporters to refer to global warming\nwhen discussing our weather. Global warming not only affects our\ncomfort level but the cost of energy and the quality of air that we\nbreathe.\n\nSeveral important facts need to be remembered when discussing the\nsolutions to global warming.\n\n-greenhouse gas emissions, created by the burning of fossil fuels in\nour cars and power plants, linger in the atmosphere for decades.\n\n-reforestation is the only post-combustion measure that can contribute\nto reducing the threat of global warming.\n\n-according to a recent study by the New York State Energy Office on\ncarbon reduction, planting of trees in urban areas is one of, if not\nthe most, cost effective measure for reducing carbon emissions. Its\ncost is a minus $9 per ton of carbon reduced.\n\nAll these pertinent facts are addressed by this bill. For years the\nstate and city has looked into the federal government to fund\nreforestation efforts. Funds in past years have dwindled to $10,000\nfor statewide efforts. We can no longer wait for other venues to fund\nthis important and cost effective measure. We should give the people\nof the City of New York the opportunity to act locally while thinking\nglobally. The tax return checkoff would enable people who wish to\nbetter their own environment as well as contribute to a worldwide\nreduction in carbon emissions the opportunity to do so without using\ngeneral fund moneys in these tough fiscal times.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\nS.910A  of 2007-2008\nS.1792A of 2005-2006\nS.271A  of 2003-2004\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nBecause this is essentially a bill to provide funding,\nthere would be little fiscal impact.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect January 1, 2012; provided,\nhowever, that the department of taxation and finance shall effectuate\nthe provisions of section of this act on or before such effective date.\n",
